From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 07:20:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] dts: pinephone: keep the modem rail powered

0001-dts-pinephone-drop-modem-power-node removed the only consumer of
reg_vbat_bb. That node carried

    power-supply = <&reg_vbat_bb>;   /* PL7 */

and with it gone nothing claims the rail, so the EG25 never gets its 3.5V:

    /sys/class/regulator: vbat-bb  state=disabled  users=0
    (vbat-wifi, for contrast, is enabled with 2 users)

eg25-manager cannot cover for it - its board config
(/usr/share/eg25-manager/pine64,pinephone-1.2.toml) drives GPIOs only
(dtr/pwrkey/reset/apready/disable/status) and has no regulator entry. So it
pulses PWRKEY into a modem that has no power, reads STATUS low, and concludes

    STATUS is low, modem already powered

after which it just waits forever:

    Rescheduling upload since modem isn't online yet, in 30s

The modem never enumerates (no 2c7c:0125 on any of usb1..usb4, all four host
controllers up and idle), so ofono has no device and the UI shows no bars.

Mark the rail always-on. This mirrors what
0004-dts-pinephone-pro-keep-the-4G-rails-powered.patch already does for the
PinePhone Pro, which is why the same EG25 works there.

Trade-off: the rail stays up whenever the phone is on, rather than following
modem power state. Doing it properly means teaching eg25-manager to own the
regulator - it has no such support today - or reinstating a DT consumer that
does not also claim the GPIOs eg25-manager needs, which is what 0001 removed the
node to avoid.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [depends on LuneOS dropping the modem-power node]
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-a64-pinephone.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-a64-pinephone.dtsi
@@ -393,6 +393,7 @@
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <3500000>;
 		gpio = <&r_pio 0 7 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>; /* PL7 */
 		enable-active-high;
+		regulator-always-on;
 	};
 
 	reg_vbat_wifi: vbat-wifi {
